==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
Cognate with {{cog|en|cheap}}, {{cog|de|kaufen}}. Common Germanic of debated etymology:
# {{der|non|gem-pro|*kaupaz|pos=n}}, from {{der|non|la|caupō||tradesman}}. Cognate with {{cog|got|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐍉𐌽||barter}}.
# Of native origin, going on {{cog|got|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽||strike, cuff}}, which is morphologically compatible with ''kaupa''. Senses in other languages derived by relating striking (of hands) to transaction, as with {{m|en|strike a bargain}}, ''{{soplink|slá|kaup|form2=kaupi|lang=non}}''.<ref>George Hempl, University of Michigan. "Etymologies - Cheap, cope, coup, kaupatjan, caupo, κάπηλος, etc." ''Modern language notes, Volume 17,'' pp. 210-212. Johns Hopkins University, JSTOR, via [http://books.google.com/books?id=hfwmAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA210 Google Books].</ref>

===Verb===
{{non-verb}}

# to [[buy]]

